  // The orchestra's music (spec section 60, ADR-071): an original piece for every mood, played by a
  // synthesized orchestra in a hall, made when the game starts. Every piece loops: its last bar leads
  // into its first. All are centred on D, so one mood crosses into another without a jolt.
  //
  // Chords: a symbol a bar (Dm, Bb, F/A, Csus4, A7, Gm7, C#dim7, Ebaug), or Dm:2 for a chord of so many
  // beats; | only reads well. A part plays its Bars (1-8,13-16; every bar when left out) as it is told:
  // - Pad: holds each chord, voiced near its Octave (4 holds middle C), the voices moving as little as
  //   they can;
  // - Bass: holds each chord's root, or its slash bass;
  // - Ostinato and Arpeggio: a Pattern of chord tones (1, 3, 5, 7 the chord's notes; 8, 10, 12 and 15 an
  //   octave or two up; -5 an octave down), a note every Step beats, short or left to ring;
  // - Melody: Notes in scientific pitch (D5 is the D above middle C) with their beats (D5:1.5; -:1 rest);
  // - Rhythm: a Pattern of steps (x a hit, X an accent, o a soft hit, r a roll, . a rest), a step every
  //   Step beats; drums are tuned to the chord, other instruments strike the chord short;
  // - Swell: a cymbal or a drum roll swelling, over Step beats, into the downbeat after each of its bars.
  // Dynamics [from, to] makes a crescendo or a fade over each run of bars. Volume is the part's level and
  // Level the piece's against the others.
